<p>I set the ceiling with max_tokens at 16. The whole output counts toward it, including tokens the reader never sees. The model spent them on reasoning. Nothing in the limit was left for a visible sentence.</p>
<p>Most language models, GPT, Claude and Gemini among them, are autoregressive. The next token depends on all the previous ones and arrives from left to right. Inside a single call those steps cannot run side by side. Mercury 3, from Inception, is a diffusion language model. It does not begin with the first word. It starts from noise and cleans that noise over a few steps until the result is coherent text. The sentence is approached from several places at once.</p>
<p>Generation can therefore be fast and the answer still empty. In its <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20261019090400/en/Inception-Launches-Mercury-3-Setting-a-New-Pareto-Frontier-in-Speed-and-Intelligence">Business Wire announcement</a> of 19 October 2026, Inception reports more than 1,000 tokens per second on widely available NVIDIA GPUs. The announcement says the model outperforms GPT-6 Luna and Claude Haiku 5.5 and runs four to eight times faster. That is their figure, not a measurement from this call. On <a href="https://openrouter.ai/inception/mercury-3">OpenRouter</a>, on 25 October 2026, the median throughput was 277 tokens per second. The announcement and the gateway card give different numbers here.</p>
<p>The price list in that same announcement has two layers. The base is $0.20 per million input tokens and $0.70 per million output tokens. At launch a fifty-percent discount applies, so $0.10 and $0.35. Cache reads are ninety percent cheaper than input. On the OpenRouter card, on 25 October 2026, that was $0.01. The announcement does not call the discount permanent.</p>
<p>OpenRouter gives a release date of 16 October 2026, a context of one million tokens and an output ceiling of 131,072. Input and output are text. The model card also lists tools and structured output. Diffusion fits that in a plain way. When the whole sequence is cleaned at once, it can be asked to land on a schema, for example valid JSON. Whether it does so on a given task has to be checked on that task.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.inceptionlabs.ai/capabilities/reasoning-efforts">Inception’s documentation</a> distinguishes four levels of the reasoning_effort parameter: instant, low, medium and high. If the parameter is omitted, mercury-3 goes to low, according to that page. A higher level spends more tokens on reasoning. It is slower and more expensive. On a hard task the higher effort belongs there. In OpenCode the level is chosen by a variant, for example inception/mercury-3#high. The <a href="https://docs.inceptionlabs.ai/usecases/voice">voice documentation</a> recommends, at medium effort, a max_completion_tokens ceiling of at least 3,000. Sixteen is below what the model spends before a sentence.</p>
<p>Long continuous writing with a strict sequence remains the ground autoregression was built on. A diffusion model may need tuning there. In the announcement Inception places it rather where waiting adds up: an agent, voice, search, support.</p>
<p>The speed of generation and the speed of a useful answer are measured apart. While the limit covers only the reasoning, the reader gets no sentence. The gap announces itself quickly.</p>
<p><em>The empty answer is from one call of my own with max_tokens 16. The speed above 1,000 tokens per second, the comparison with GPT-6 Luna and Claude Haiku 5.5, and the price list are from Inception’s Business Wire announcement of 19 October 2026. The OpenRouter card and the reasoning_effort documentation were read on 25 October 2026. Prices change.</em></p>

